# Whether each side's arm says it holds a part (ADR-0072; review R-01).
#
# A run opens with a release, and a release on an arm that holds a part drops it
# where the arm stands, so before its first step a run reads the custody of every
# side its target commands. The plant's is on the program's own domain; any
# other side's is on that side's, which no client opens (ADR-0044 clause 3,
# ADR-0066, ADR-0071). So it is asked of the boundary, which reads each side's
# latched `RobotState` (the skill server's `state`, per arm the plan names) on
# that side's own domain, as it reads each side's joint states for JointsAt.
#
# The boundary answers a verdict per side and not the message: the reverse state
# flow is consumed by L5 and republished nowhere (ADR-0050 decision 1b). A side
# with an arm whose state was never heard is `heard` false, and a caller refuses
# it: an unheard custody is never assumed empty.
#
# SERVICE is the one place this name is written, as SetMode's is.

string SERVICE="/cite/twin/holding"

string[] sides      # the sides to answer for, by the plan's side names; empty answers
                    # every side the deployment runs. A side named that does not run
                    # is answered unheard
---
string[] sides      # the sides answered, in the order asked
bool[] heard        # per side: a RobotState has been heard from every arm on it
bool[] holding      # per side: an arm on it says it holds a part (gripper_holding)
string[] detail     # per side: what it holds, or which arm is unheard; empty if neither
